nodecode-lastscanned is a rewrite of dragonhuntr's dragonhuntr/lastscanned-plugin:

  https://github.com/dragonhuntr/lastscanned-plugin
  commit 7d51abaf682fd21c2efa611ef4d51f415e282548
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 dragonhuntr (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the whole repository at that commit: a portable Agent
Plugins v1 package with no program of its own -- one Streamable HTTP MCP
server at https://mcp.lastscanned.com/mcp, declared in mcp.json, and one
skill in skills/lastscanned/SKILL.md. All four of the server's tools are
carried over with their names and their arguments: track_package and
track_packages (a tracking number or an ocean container number, with an
optional carrier id), get_rates (USPS, UPS and FedEx quotes between two
postal codes, with weight, weight unit, residential, dimensions and their
unit, carriers, and the two country codes) and list_carriers. The wire is
MCP's streamable HTTP as the plugin declares it: one POST per JSON-RPC
message to the endpoint, initialize and the initialized notification once
per start with the Mcp-Session-Id echoed on every later request, and an
answer that is either one JSON body or an SSE stream whose data: lines carry
the message -- the framing the plugin's skill never had to speak, because a
client spoke it for the plugin. The skill's guidance is the cell's manual,
(help :lastscanned): the four tools and what each takes, that a tracking
number's carrier is detected unless it is named, the ocean lines that work
and that a leased container needs its line named, that a quote buys nothing,
that delivered on a container means the empty box came back, and that each
tracked number and each quote counts toward the account. LICENSE is the
repository's LICENSE, unchanged.

What was left out, and why: Agent Plugins v1 itself (plugin.json, mcp.json
and the skill packaging), because a cell is a Lisp system and the section
below it is its manifest; the OAuth sign-in that documentation/NATIVE-SETUP.md
offers, because a browser grant needs a callback this cell does not host, so
the cell takes an API key -- written, named by an environment variable, or
none at all for the free daily allowance; the readOnlyHint note, which in
Hermes skips an approval prompt the cell does not have, so the manual says
instead that a call counts toward the account; and the REST API's watches and
webhooks, which the plugin did not ship either. Python and the MCP client
library it would have used are replaced by the image's own nlk:http.
